  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday: The Inland NW will remain in a somewhat \nactive pattern, with periodic shower and thunderstorm chances and \ntemperatures a bit closer to seasonal norms. Highs will be in \nmid-80s through mid-90s this weekend. A baggy upper trough \nretrogrades west, but impulses and lingering moisture (with PWATs \naround 120-160% of normal) will work with daily daytime \ninstability to bring these precipitation chances. Today a \nshortwave swinging in will spread a threat of showers and \nthunderstorms across the region. The best chances will be around \nthe Cascades and mountains, but other chances will also expand \nacross the Columbia Basin in more isolated to scattered fashion. \nSome of these could contain brief heavy rain and gusty winds, with\nthe best risk around the mountain zones and southeast CWA again. \nSmaller chances will be found Sunday, found in spotty fashion \naround the Cascades and northern mountains and potentially a bit \nmore coverage near the far southeast CWA, especially later in the \nday as a wave surges along the boundary stalled in that region. \n\nAs for fire weather and lightning and wind threat, a modest \nchance for thunderstorms will be found near the Cascades again \nthis afternoon and evening; elevated fire weather conditions are \nforecast but there is a better chance for wet thunderstorms and \nlimit the fire threat. A Red Flag Warning was issued for FWZ709, \nnear the Blue Mountains from noon to 9 pm Saturday, where there is\nhigher confidence of abundant lightning and it is an area that \nhas not see much rain. Gusty outflow winds from any t-storms could\npost a risk for spread on any new or ongoing wildfires.\n\nMonday to Friday: The offshore trough pushes back into the Monday\ninto Tuesday, but it looks weak, while additional weak waves into\nthe end of the week. Models have backed of on the coverage of\npotential Monday to Thursday the main shower chances retreat to\nthe mountains and look more isolated in coverage. Some better\nchances arrive toward the end of the week. Temperature will be a\nmore seasonal 80s to low 90s. Winds do not look to impactful at\nthis time, but we will have to watch for some gaps winds near the\nCascades and down the Okanogan Valley at times. /Solveig",
